From 1a2b3c4d5e6f7a8b9c0d1e2f3a4b5c6d7e8f9a0b Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: DockSeed <dockseed@proton.me>
Date: Tue, 21 Jul 2026 14:00:00 +0200
Subject: [PATCH v3] arm64: dts: a733: add the core-level PSCI idle states, one
 per cluster
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Add a core power-down idle state for each cluster and point every cpu@N
at its own: cpu0-cpu5 (Cortex-A55) at cpu-sleep-0-a55, cpu6-cpu7
(Cortex-A76) at cpu-sleep-0-a76. Both carry the same PSCI parameter;
they differ in how long the core takes to come back.

v3: the latencies are measured on this boot chain instead of
copied from the vendor device tree, and the two clusters differ, so the
one state becomes two. v2: the cluster-level state is gone, see
CLUSTER STATE below.

THE NUMBERS (us)

                      entry   exit   entry+exit   min-residency
  cpu-sleep-0-a55       50     150       200          3570
  cpu-sleep-0-a76       50     210       260          3570
  v2, both (vendor)     46      59       105          3570

Linux never uses entry and exit on their own. With no wakeup-latency-us,
dt_idle_states.c adds them, and the sum is what cpuidle compares with a
PM QoS limit, what /sys/devices/system/cpu/cpuN/cpuidle/state1/latency
reads, and what idle injection filters on (`arm64-dts-a733-idle-injection-cooling`: the exit-latency-us of
a thermal-idle node is a limit on this sum, so admitting the deep state
takes >= 200 on cpu2-cpu5 and >= 260 on cpu6-cpu7).

HOW THEY WERE MEASURED. 8 GiB board (DRAM trained at 2040 MHz),
2026-09-24, default boot chain (native CPU_SUSPEND), this kernel with
the v2 DT; instruments: our own test programs
(a733-idle-wake.c, a733-idle-latency.py; not part of this recipe).
Checked on the 6 GiB board (DRAM at 2400 MHz) with the same instruments: inside every bound below (A55 exit max
144 us, A76 203 us; worst wake 167 / 234 us).

  * A thread pinned to the target core blocks on an eventfd; another
    core wakes it at a random delay d after the target went idle, with
    cpuidle state0 disabled on the target so that it takes the deep
    state. From ftrace (trace_clock mono): d = sched_waking minus the
    target's cpu_idle entry, latency = the target's cpu_idle exit minus
    sched_waking.
  * Both clusters pinned to the lowest OPP, 247 MHz. The wake path is
    mostly software -- BL31 warm boot, context restore, cpu_pm
    notifiers -- and runs at the core clock: at the highest OPP the same
    wake takes 61 us on the A55 and about 120 us on the A76. The lowest
    OPP is the worst case, so that is what the DT carries.
  * exit = the fully entered state (d >= 60 us), maximum over two runs,
    rounded up to 5 us: A55 147 -> 150 (p50 124, p99 129-132, n=6439,
    cpu2 and cpu4), A76 208 -> 210 (p50 181-196, p99 204-205, n=5076,
    cpu6 and cpu7).
  * entry. A wake that lands while the core is still going down waits
    for the rest of the entry and then the whole exit, so latency + d
    stays constant until the core is down: 172 us on the A55 from
    d = 8 to 48 us, about 246 us on the A76 from d = 12 to 48 us. From
    d = 48 us on, both sit on their exit plateau. Earlier than ~8 us
    (A55) / ~12 us (A76) the wake aborts the entry instead: BL31 checks
    ISR_EL1 before it commits (psci_cpu_suspend_start()) and returns
    without powering down, in 21-32 us. entry = 50 for both.
  * So entry + exit bounds every wake measured this way (worst: A55
    171 us, A76 242 us). A timer wake is slower by one hop: this DT has
    no broadcast timer, so a core in this state (local-timer-stop) is
    woken by another core's broadcast hrtimer. Measured with
    cyclictest --laptop at 20 ms, lowest OPP: p99 169-183 us (A55) and
    232-236 us (A76), inside the bounds; one A55 timer wake of 1904 took
    207 us.

min-residency-us stays the vendor's 3570 us. It is an energy break-even,
not a latency, and no trace shows it; an A/B on the 6 GiB board
(2026-09-24) against 1000 us found no difference in idle power (means
within 12 mW of 4.07 W over four arms). Labelled as the vendor's value.

What is spec-firm: the encoding of arm,psci-suspend-param itself, per
the PSCI specification (ARM DEN 0022) original power-state format --
  0x00010000 = state-type 1 (power-down), power level 0 (core)
These are the standard field positions, not vendor-invented values, and
the format is the one our firmware answers PSCI_FEATURES(CPU_SUSPEND)
with (TF-A builds with PSCI_EXTENDED_STATE_ID = 0).

WHY TWO NODES AND NOT ONE. cpuidle-psci registers one driver per CPU
(psci_idle_init_cpu(): drv->cpumask = cpumask_of(cpu)), and
dt_init_idle_driver() only requires the states to agree across that
driver's mask, so each cluster can point at its own node. The node name
becomes the state name in sysfs.

CLUSTER STATE (v2). v1 also declared cluster-sleep-0,
0x01010000 = power-down at power level 1. The encoding was right: TF-A
builds the A733 as root / one cluster / eight cores (PLAT_MAX_PWR_LVL 2,
plat_power_domain_tree_desc in plat/allwinner/common/sunxi_topology.c),
so level 1 is the DSU cluster, and 0x01010000 passes the SBZ mask
(PSTATE_VALID_MASK 0xFCFE0000). The vendor BL31 v2.5 entered it. Our
BL31 does not implement a cluster power-down: its validate_power_state
refuses every level other than 0 with PSCI_E_INVALID_PARAMS
(the TF-A patch `allwinner-a733-native-psci-cpu-suspend`, sunxi_validate_power_state). That refusal is correct,
and it is where v1 went wrong: it assumed a rejected
state is merely "not used". It is used on every idle entry. cpuidle
counts the rejection and returns without WFI, do_idle() asks the
governor again, and nothing in the kernel retires a state that keeps
failing. Measured on our chain (2026-09-23): 1.4-2.6 million
refused CPU_SUSPEND calls per core in about 40 s, one every ~10 us --
an idle loop that spins through cpu_pm_enter(), the context save and an
SMC round trip instead of sleeping. The DT and the BL31 ship together,
so the DT declares what the BL31 implements: core level only. The
cluster state comes back in the same change that teaches the firmware
a cluster power-down (DSU PPU, L3 maintenance, GIC-600 stream gating).

Depends on patch `arm64-dts-a733-cpu-topology` (cpu topology) and sits on top of `arm64-dts-a733-heterogeneous-pmu`. No driver
code: cpuidle plus PSCI firmware do the work once the DT describes the
states. `arm64-dts-a733-cache-geometry` carries the cpu-idle-states lines as context and is
regenerated with this version.

VERIFIED THIS PASS:
  * patch -p1 --fuzz=0 clean against `clk-sunxi-ng-add-A733-sun60iw2-minimal-ccu` to `arm64-dts-a733-heterogeneous-pmu`, and the full series
    applies at fuzz 0 with `arm64-dts-a733-cache-geometry` regenerated
  * dtc: /cpus/idle-states holds cpu-sleep-0-a55 and cpu-sleep-0-a76,
    cpu@0..cpu@500 reference the first, cpu@600/cpu@700 the second
  * silicon, 8 GiB board, 2026-09-24: the default kernel booted with
    this dtb as its own label reads state1 = cpu-sleep-0-a55, latency 200
    on cpu0-cpu5 and cpu-sleep-0-a76, latency 260 on cpu6-cpu7; usage
    rises on all eight cores over 60 s idle. The measurement above was
    repeated on this DT (the second of the two runs).
  * the dtb was built locally: the change is DT only, and the same local
    build of main reproduces the test build's dtb byte for byte

PENDING:
  * when this was written, the thermal-idle limit in
    `arm64-dts-a733-idle-injection-cooling` read 100 and kept idle
    injection on WFI; that patch now uses 500, which admits both states.

DELIBERATELY OMITTED:
  * thermal-idle sub-nodes (BSP carries them on cpu@2-7) -- part of the
    thermal cooling machinery, which is `arm64-dts-a733-idle-injection-cooling`.

Assisted-by: Claude
---
 .../sun60i-a733-cubie-a7s-minimal.dts         | 48 +++++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 48 insertions(+)

diff --git a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/allwinner/sun60i-a733-cubie-a7s-minimal.dts b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/allwinner/sun60i-a733-cubie-a7s-minimal.dts
--- a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/allwinner/sun60i-a733-cubie-a7s-minimal.dts
+++ b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/allwinner/sun60i-a733-cubie-a7s-minimal.dts
@@ -55,6 +55,7 @@
 			reg = <0x000>;
 			enable-method = "psci";
 			next-level-cache = <&L2_0>;
+			cpu-idle-states = <&cpu_sleep_0_a55>;
 		};
 
 		cpu1: cpu@100 {
@@ -63,6 +64,7 @@
 			reg = <0x100>;
 			enable-method = "psci";
 			next-level-cache = <&L2_1>;
+			cpu-idle-states = <&cpu_sleep_0_a55>;
 		};
 
 		cpu2: cpu@200 {
@@ -71,6 +73,7 @@
 			reg = <0x200>;
 			enable-method = "psci";
 			next-level-cache = <&L2_2>;
+			cpu-idle-states = <&cpu_sleep_0_a55>;
 		};
 
 		cpu3: cpu@300 {
@@ -79,6 +82,7 @@
 			reg = <0x300>;
 			enable-method = "psci";
 			next-level-cache = <&L2_3>;
+			cpu-idle-states = <&cpu_sleep_0_a55>;
 		};
 
 		cpu4: cpu@400 {
@@ -87,6 +91,7 @@
 			reg = <0x400>;
 			enable-method = "psci";
 			next-level-cache = <&L2_4>;
+			cpu-idle-states = <&cpu_sleep_0_a55>;
 		};
 
 		cpu5: cpu@500 {
@@ -95,6 +100,7 @@
 			reg = <0x500>;
 			enable-method = "psci";
 			next-level-cache = <&L2_5>;
+			cpu-idle-states = <&cpu_sleep_0_a55>;
 		};
 
 		/*
@@ -114,6 +120,7 @@
 			reg = <0x600>;
 			enable-method = "psci";
 			next-level-cache = <&L2_6>;
+			cpu-idle-states = <&cpu_sleep_0_a76>;
 		};
 
 		cpu7: cpu@700 {
@@ -122,6 +129,7 @@
 			reg = <0x700>;
 			enable-method = "psci";
 			next-level-cache = <&L2_7>;
+			cpu-idle-states = <&cpu_sleep_0_a76>;
 		};
 
 		cpu-map {
@@ -139,6 +147,46 @@
 				core1 { cpu = <&cpu7>; };
 			};
 		};
+
+		/*
+		 * PSCI-managed idle states: one core power-down state per
+		 * cluster. Both use the same PSCI parameter; they differ in how
+		 * long the core takes to come back, and cpuidle, PM QoS and idle
+		 * injection all compare against entry + exit. The latencies are
+		 * measured on this boot chain at the lowest OPP, where the wake
+		 * path is slowest; min-residency-us is the vendor's value, kept
+		 * because an A/B against 1000 us found no difference in idle
+		 * power. Method and numbers: this patch's commit message.
+		 *
+		 * Core level only. Our BL31 implements no cluster power-down and
+		 * refuses a level-1 power_state (0x01010000) with INVALID_PARAMS;
+		 * cpuidle never retires a refused state, it asks for it again on
+		 * every idle entry. A cluster state is declared here together
+		 * with the firmware that implements it, not before.
+		 */
+		idle-states {
+			entry-method = "psci";
+
+			/* cpu0-cpu5, Cortex-A55 */
+			cpu_sleep_0_a55: cpu-sleep-0-a55 {
+				compatible = "arm,idle-state";
+				local-timer-stop;
+				entry-latency-us = <50>;
+				exit-latency-us = <150>;
+				min-residency-us = <3570>;
+				arm,psci-suspend-param = <0x00010000>;
+			};
+
+			/* cpu6-cpu7, Cortex-A76 */
+			cpu_sleep_0_a76: cpu-sleep-0-a76 {
+				compatible = "arm,idle-state";
+				local-timer-stop;
+				entry-latency-us = <50>;
+				exit-latency-us = <210>;
+				min-residency-us = <3570>;
+				arm,psci-suspend-param = <0x00010000>;
+			};
+		};
 	};
 
 	/*
